---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ForecastChart from '../components/ForecastChart.astro';
import DataNotice from '../components/DataNotice.astro';
import { env } from 'cloudflare:workers';
import { getData } from '../lib/data';
import { seasonLabel, formatDate } from '../lib/enso';
import {
  ArrowUpRight,
  ArrowRight,
  GitCompareArrows,
  CalendarDays,
  ShieldAlert,
  Info,
  Compass,
  CloudRain,
  Snowflake,
  Activity
} from '@lucide/astro';

const { snapshot, stale } = await getData(env);
const forecast = snapshot.forecast.data;
const target = forecast.rows.find(row => row.season === 'DJF') ?? forecast.rows[0];
const previous = snapshot.previous;
const priorTarget = previous?.rows.find(row => row.key === target.key);
const delta = priorTarget ? target.nino - priorTarget.nino : null;

// Multi-season comparison rows matching identical target keys across consecutive monthly issues
const comparisonRows = forecast.rows.map(row => {
  const prev = previous?.rows.find(p => p.key === row.key);
  const diff = prev !== undefined ? row.nino - prev.nino : null;
  return {
    ...row,
    label: seasonLabel(row),
    prevNino: prev?.nino ?? null,
    delta: diff
  };
});
---
<Layout title="El Niño & La Niña Forecast: ENSO Probabilities" active="forecast" description="Compare NOAA CPC El Niño, neutral and La Niña probabilities by season, changes since the previous issue, and dated El Niño strength estimates.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage">
    <div class="page-intro">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">SEASONAL PROBABILITIES</span>
        <h1>El Niño & La Niña <span>forecast.</span></h1>
        <p>NOAA CPC issue {forecast.issue} gives {seasonLabel(target)} probabilities of {target.nino}% El Niño, {target.neutral}% neutral and {target.nina}% La Niña. Compare seasonal targets and changes since the previous issue below.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <DataNotice stale={stale} />

    <div class="content-notice">
      <CalendarDays size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>Next scheduled consensus release: {formatDate(snapshot.discussion.data.next, { month: 'long' })}.</strong>
        <p>CPC issues full diagnostic discussions and updated seasonal ENSO category probabilities on the second Thursday of each month. Current figures reflect issue {forecast.issue}.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Active Winter Highlight -->
    <div class="forecast-summary">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">{seasonLabel(target)} OUTLOOK</span>
        <div class="forecast-big">{target.nino}<span>%</span></div>
        <p>El Niño probability · {seasonLabel(target)}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="forecast-revision">
        <GitCompareArrows size={23} />
        <div>
          <strong>{delta === null ? 'Comparison unavailable' : (delta > 0 ? '+' : '') + delta + ' percentage points'}</strong>
          <p>{priorTarget ? `${priorTarget.nino}% in ${snapshot.previous!.issue} to ${target.nino}% in ${forecast.issue}. Both estimates refer to ${seasonLabel(target)}.` : 'No verified previous estimate exists for this target season.'}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="wmo-record-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">DATED UPDATE · WMO RELEASE NOVEMBER 9, 2026</span>
      <h2 id="wmo-record-heading">Will this El Niño be the strongest on record?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">In its November 9, 2026 climate update, the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) said El Niño <q>is expected to be the strongest on record by December</q>. This is WMO's forecast statement about December 2026, not confirmation that an observed seasonal record has already been broken.</p>
      <h3>What does the record claim measure?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">The quoted sentence in WMO's press release does not specify the ENSO index, dataset version or averaging period used for the record comparison. ENSOLens therefore attributes the statement to WMO rather than assigning it an ONI or RONI threshold, a probability, or a new historical ranking. A weekly Niño 3.4 value alone cannot verify a seasonal event-strength record.</p>
      <h3>How does this relate to the CPC forecast below?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">The chart below reports NOAA CPC's dated probabilities for El Niño, neutral and La Niña. The separate strength table preserves CPC's October 2026 RONI forecast. Neither product is a numerical translation of WMO's record statement, and the WMO release does not update those CPC figures.</p>
      <h3>Does it predict the weather where I live?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">WMO expects El Niño to continue into early 2027 and describes added heat and climate risks against an already warm global background. That does not specify a city's storm dates, snowfall or flood probability. Use the <a href="/winter-outlook/">dated U.S. winter outlook</a> for regional seasonal context and <a href="https://www.weather.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the National Weather Service</a> for local forecasts and warnings.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">Source: <a href="https://wmo.int/news/media-centre/state-of-climate-2026-gives-new-meaning-phrase-charts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WMO, “State of the climate in 2026 gives new meaning to phrase ‘off the charts’” <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a>, issued November 9; checked November 10, 2026. This dated interpretation remains separate from the automatically refreshed CPC chart.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="body-copy">Reading the three possible outcomes? <a href="/forecast/enso-neutral/">ENSO-neutral does not mean normal local weather.</a> For regional context, browse the <a href="/impacts/">U.S. impact guides</a>.</p>
    <!-- Interactive Probability Chart -->
    <ForecastChart expanded snapshot={snapshot} />

    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="reading-probabilities-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">READING THE ENSO FORECAST</span>
      <h2 id="reading-probabilities-heading">How to read El Niño, neutral and La Niña probabilities</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">Read all three probabilities in one row, together with the issue date and target season. They describe alternative ENSO states for that season. They are not the share of days in each state, the percentage of forecast models voting for it, or the chance of a local weather impact.</p>
      <h3>Does the largest probability mean more likely than not?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">Not always. In a <strong>teaching example, not a current CPC forecast</strong>, El Niño at 45%, neutral at 40% and La Niña at 15% makes El Niño the most likely single category. But the other two categories together have a 55% chance. The leading category need not have a majority; retain the alternatives when describing the outlook.</p>
      <h3>Why do the target seasons overlap?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">DJF means December–January–February; JFM means January–February–March. They share January and February, so adjacent rows are related seasonal windows, not independent monthly forecasts. Do not add their probabilities or interpret a difference between rows as the change expected in one particular month. Keep the year in view when a season crosses December.</p>
      <h3>What counts as a month-to-month forecast change?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">Compare the same target season and year across two issue dates. A teaching example of 60% becoming 70% is a rise of <strong>10 percentage points</strong>, not 10%. A lower El Niño probability does not by itself mean La Niña is next: check whether neutral, La Niña or both gained probability. The <a href="#comparison-heading">fixed-target comparison</a> below preserves missing estimates instead of treating them as zero.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">CPC combines observations and multiple models in an expert assessment. Its published 0% is approximate rather than an absolute impossibility. Phase probability also differs from <a href="#strength-heading">event-strength probability</a>; for local seasonal weather, use the <a href="/winter-outlook/">regional outlook</a>.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Definitions checked November 12, 2026: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/probabilities/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CPC ENSO probabilities</a>. The numerical teaching examples above remain separate from the live official table.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="super-el-nino-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">READING ENSO STRENGTH</span>
      <h2 id="super-el-nino-heading">What is a super El Niño?</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">“Super El Niño” is a shorthand for an unusually intense event. It is not a separate category in CPC's strength-probability product, which uses weak, moderate, strong and very strong. Before comparing a headline with a measurement, check the index, averaging period and reference baseline.</p>
      <h3>Can one very warm week establish event strength?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">No. A weekly Niño 3.4 anomaly measures one week's sea-surface temperature departure. CPC's strength probabilities refer to three-month RONI values. Even a weekly reading above +3°C cannot be placed directly into a seasonal RONI category or used on its own to declare a record event.</p>
      <h3>Why do ONI and RONI give different numbers?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">ONI averages Niño 3.4 temperature anomalies over three months. RONI also accounts for the tropical ocean background: it subtracts the tropical-mean anomaly and applies an adjustment to match the variance of the original Niño 3.4 index. It is not simply a weekly value with a fixed amount subtracted. Compare historical events using the same index, dataset version, baseline method and averaging period; recent seasonal estimates can be revised.</p>
      <h3>Does a stronger event guarantee worse local weather?</h3>
      <p class="body-copy">No. Event strength describes the tropical Pacific signal. It does not give your city's chance of flooding, a cold outbreak or heavy snow. Use regional seasonal outlooks for temperature and precipitation probabilities, then local forecasts for individual storms.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Definitions checked November 10, 2026: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/strengths/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CPC strength categories</a>, <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RONI method</a> and <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/oni/v6/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ONI method</a>. See also <a href="/faq/#super-el-nino">the ENSO FAQ</a> and <a href="/winter-outlook/">the dated U.S. winter outlook</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="strength-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">STRENGTH EXPLAINER · CPC ISSUE OCTOBER 2026</span>
      <h2 id="strength-heading">El Niño odds are not strength odds.</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">In CPC's October 2026 issue, the December 2026–February 2027 El Niño probability is 100%, while the probability of the very strong category is 81%. The first includes every El Niño strength; the second describes just one intensity range. Neither is a probability of extreme weather in your city.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">Reviewed November 8, 2026. The percentages below refer to all outcomes, not just cases where El Niño occurs.</p>
      <div class="probability-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Dated ENSO strength probabilities" tabindex="0">
        <table class="data-table" id="strength-table">
          <caption>CPC October 2026 · dated reference</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Target season</th><th scope="col">Weak<br />0.5 ≤ RONI &lt; 1.0°C</th><th scope="col">Moderate<br />1.0 ≤ RONI &lt; 1.5°C</th><th scope="col">Strong<br />1.5 ≤ RONI &lt; 2.0°C</th><th scope="col">Very strong<br />RONI ≥ 2.0°C</th><th scope="col">All El Niño</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">DJF 2026–27</th><td>0%</td><td>3%</td><td>16%</td><td>81%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">JFM 2027</th><td>2%</td><td>15%</td><td>37%</td><td>46%</td><td>100%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-note">RONI is the three-month Relative Oceanic Niño Index, not the weekly Niño 3.4 anomaly. CPC uses the 1991–2020 base period for this product. JFM means January–March. Published probabilities are approximate; 0% is not an absolute impossibility.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">For January–March 2027, the strong and very strong categories together total 83% (37% + 46%). That is different from the 46% chance of very strong alone. A strength category describes the tropical Pacific signal, not a guaranteed regional impact.</p>
      <p class="figure-note">This manually reviewed October reference stays fixed when the live phase chart updates. <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/strengths/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Check CPC's current strength issue <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a>. For regional temperature and precipitation, see the <a href="/winter-outlook/">dated U.S. winter outlook</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1. Full Multi-Season Fixed-Target Comparison Table -->
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">MONTH-OVER-MONTH REVISIONS</span>
          <h2 id="comparison-heading">Fixed-target season comparison</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="body-copy">
        A genuine forecast change occurs only when probability estimates shift for the <strong>exact same target season</strong> across monthly releases. Comparing a winter target with a summer target compares different periods, not a revision to the same forecast.
      </p>
      <div class="probability-table-wrap">
        <table class="data-table">
          <caption>NOAA CPC · {previous ? `Comparing issues ${previous.issue} and ${forecast.issue} for identical target seasons` : `Issue ${forecast.issue}; previous issue unavailable`}</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Target Season</th>
              <th scope="col">{previous?.issue ?? 'Previous'} El Niño</th>
              <th scope="col">{forecast.issue} El Niño</th>
              <th scope="col">Net Shift</th>
              <th scope="col">Neutral</th>
              <th scope="col">La Niña</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {comparisonRows.map(row => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
                <td>{row.prevNino !== null ? `${row.prevNino}%` : '—'}</td>
                <td class="warm-text">{row.nino}%</td>
                <td>
                  {row.delta === null ? (
                    <span class="subtle-text">Comparison unavailable</span>
                  ) : row.delta > 0 ? (
                    <span class="warm-text">+{row.delta} pp</span>
                  ) : row.delta < 0 ? (
                    <span class="cold-text">{row.delta} pp</span>
                  ) : (
                    <span class="subtle-text">±0 pp</span>
                  )}
                </td>
                <td>{row.neutral}%</td>
                <td>{row.nina}%</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="figure-note">
        Comparison is unavailable when the previous issue or a matching target season is missing. Missing estimates are never treated as 0% probability. Net shift is measured in percentage points (pp).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Interpretation Boundaries: What seasonal probabilities CANNOT decide -->
    <section class="regional-section" aria-labelledby="boundaries-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">INTERPRETATION BOUNDARIES</span>
      <h2 id="boundaries-heading">What seasonal probabilities cannot decide</h2>
      <div class="regional-grid">
        <article>
          <ShieldAlert size={20} />
          <h3>Not a daily weather forecast</h3>
          <p>A seasonal probability describes an overlapping three-calendar-month period across the tropical Pacific. It is not an odds rating for daily precipitation, freezing levels, or severe weather in your city.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <Info size={20} />
          <h3>100% is not zero uncertainty</h3>
          <p>These are CPC's official ENSO category probabilities, not counts of models in agreement. A displayed 100% is the published estimate for that issue and target season; new observations can lead to revisions. It does not specify event strength or guarantee local weather outcomes.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <Compass size={20} />
          <h3>Not a uniform regional script</h3>
          <p>Tropical heat redistributes global jet streams probabilistically. As 1997–98 versus 2015–16 showed in California, similar ocean anomalies can produce sharply different regional winter precipitation outcomes.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Actionable Next Steps: Choose by your decision -->
    <section class="lifecycle-section" aria-labelledby="routing-heading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">DECISION ROUTING</span>
          <h2 id="routing-heading">Where to look next: choose by your decision</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="body-copy">
        A seasonal ENSO probability is a starting point, not an operational instruction. Select the specialized official tool or deep-dive matching your operational need:
      </p>
      <div class="scenario-grid">
        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div>
            <span class="scenario-badge"><CloudRain size={13} /> 1–7 Day Storm Safety</span>
            <h3>Local Weather & Warnings</h3>
            <p>For immediate precipitation, high winds, snow levels, flash flood watches, or winter storm warnings, consult your local National Weather Service office. ENSO outlooks cannot resolve individual storm timing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-footer">
            <span>National Weather Service</span>
            <a href="https://www.weather.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Find local NWS <ArrowUpRight size={13} /></a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div>
            <span class="scenario-badge"><Snowflake size={13} /> Winter Decisions</span>
            <h3>California: Snow vs. Rain?</h3>
            <p>Planning for Sierra snowpack, ski visits to Tahoe or Mammoth, or flood preparedness? Understand why atmospheric rivers can raise freezing levels and bring rain to lower elevations even in a wet winter.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-footer">
            <span>ENSOLens Regional Guide</span>
            <a href="/impacts/california-snow-vs-rain/">Open California guide <ArrowRight size={13} /></a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div>
            <span class="scenario-badge"><CalendarDays size={13} /> Peak Timing & Decay</span>
            <h3>When Will El Niño Peak & End?</h3>
            <p>Separate the ocean-temperature peak from the official return to neutral and downstream impacts. Review why winter peaks are common, how subsurface heat depletes, and how the spring barrier affects summer odds.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-footer">
            <span>ENSOLens Lifecycle Guide</span>
            <a href="/forecast/when-will-it-end/">Read lifecycle explanation <ArrowRight size={13} /></a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="scenario-card">
          <div>
            <span class="scenario-badge"><Activity size={13} /> 3-Month U.S. Outlooks</span>
            <h3>CPC Regional Climate Maps</h3>
            <p>Looking for 3-month continental U.S. temperature and precipitation shifts? Explore NOAA CPC's official seasonal outlook maps and our overview of the split-flow jet stream configuration.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-footer">
            <span>U.S. Winter Analysis</span>
            <a href="/winter-outlook/">View winter outlook <ArrowRight size={13} /></a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Source Attribution -->
    <div class="source-callout">
      <CalendarDays size={20} />
      <div>
        <strong>Original probabilities: NOAA / CPC.</strong>
        <p>Probabilities use CPC's Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI) framework. ENSOLens tracks and contextualizes official releases; it does not produce its own proprietary forecast.</p>
      </div>
      <a href={forecast.source} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open CPC probabilities <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <script>import { initCharts } from '../scripts/charts'; initCharts();</script>
</Layout>
<style>
  #strength-heading, #super-el-nino-heading, #wmo-record-heading, #reading-probabilities-heading, #comparison-heading { scroll-margin-top: 100px; }
  #strength-table { min-width: 720px; }
  #strength-table th, #strength-table td { white-space: nowrap; }
  section[aria-labelledby="strength-heading"] .figure-note,
  section[aria-labelledby="reading-probabilities-heading"] .figure-note,
  section[aria-labelledby="super-el-nino-heading"] .figure-note { display: block; }
</style>
